.TH FKSPLIT 3 2026-09-24 "fkstring" "fkstring Library Manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
fksplit, fkarraydestroy \- split an fkstring on a delimiter byte
.SH LIBRARY
fkstring library
.RI ( libfkstring ,\~ \-lfkstring )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fkstring.h>
.PP
.BI "fkstring **fksplit(const fkstring *" src ", char " delim );
.BI "void fkarraydestroy(fkstring **" fka );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR fksplit ()
splits
.I src
at every occurrence of the byte
.IR delim ,
which is not included in the parts.
It returns a newly allocated array of new
.BR fkstring s,
terminated by a NULL pointer.
.PP
Every delimiter separates two parts, so
.I n
delimiters always yield
.IR n "\~+\~1"
parts, some of which may be empty:
\(dqa,,b\(dq yields \(dqa\(dq, \(dq\(dq, and \(dqb\(dq, and an empty
.I src
yields a single empty part.
.I src
is not modified.
.PP
.BR fkarraydestroy ()
frees every
.B fkstring
in the NULL-terminated array
.IR fka ,
then the array itself.
A NULL
.I fka
is ignored.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR fksplit ()
returns the array, or NULL if
.I src
is NULL.
.SH ERRORS
.BR fksplit ()
does not return on allocation failure; see
.BR fkstring (3).
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
fkstring *s = fkstrnew("a,b,c");
fkstring **parts = fksplit(s, \(aq,\(aq);    /* {"a", "b", "c", NULL} */
\&
for (size_t i = 0; parts[i] != NULL; i++)
    puts(fkcstr(parts[i]));
fkarraydestroy(parts);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fkstring (3),
.BR fkjoin (3),
.BR fksubstr (3)
